<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chahar Mongols</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chahars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chahar are a Mongol people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>. In the 16th century they formed one of the six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a>'s restored Mongol realm and were the personal following of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>. Their ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ligdan-khan">Ligdan Khan</a> tried to reunite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> by force in the 1620s but was driven west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>, and in 1635 his son submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, bringing the Chahar into the Qing state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chahar-mongols.jpg" alt="Chahar Mongols" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esiymbro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_Subgroups_Chakhar_1911.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Chahars began as one of the estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, centred on Jingzhao, the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. In the 15th century they moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> into the south-eastern lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a> on the Mongolian Plateau. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayan-khan">Dayan Khan</a> they became one of the six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">tumens</a>, and as his successors led them, they formed the personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Northern Yuan rulers. Pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a>, they moved east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> under Daraisung Guden Khan in the middle of the 16th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ligdan-khan">Ligdan Khan</a> died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, and his son Ejei, after submitting to the Later Jin, was made a prince; the Inner Mongolian nobility became closely tied to the Qing imperial house and married into it widely. Ejei died in 1641 and was followed by his brother Abunai. Relations stayed good until 1663, when Makata gege, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> married to the Chahar prince, died. Abunai showed his discontent with Qing rule and was placed under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a> in 1669, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> gave his title to his son Borni.</p><p>In 1675, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</a>, Abunai and his brother Lubuzung rose against the Qing with 3,000 Chahar followers. The revolt was put down within two months, and in a battle on 20 April 1675 Abunai and his followers were killed. The title was abolished, every male of the Chahar royal house was executed, even those born to Qing princesses, and the royal women other than the princesses were sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>After the revolt the Chahars were reorganised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a> of Chahar, moved to the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou">Zhangjiakou</a> and placed under the emperor's direct control rather than in a league. After the fall of the Dzungar Khanate around 1758, some were resettled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a>, where they mixed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> people and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a>. In 1911 a hundred households under the former vice-governor Sumya fled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and their descendants are the Tsahar of Selenge. Today the Chahars speak Chakhar Mongolian and live mainly in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chahars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Hong Taiji brought in Chinese officials, subdued Korea and the <b>Chahar Mongols</b>, renamed his people Manchu in 1635 and in 1636 proclaimed the Qing Empire, which conquered China from 1644.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-1616-1636">Later Jin (1616-1636)</a></li><li><blockquote>Ligdan Khan of the <b>Chahar</b> overran their lands and Guihua in 1627 to 1628; when Hong Taiji's campaign of 1632 drove him west, the Tümed soon submitted to the Later Jin (1616-1636).</blockquote><a href="/state/tumed-altan-khan">Tumed (Altan Khan)</a></li></ul></section>
